# Pictures: `screenshot` saves the screen as a BMP, PNG or GIF, `record` films it, `view` shows a picture
# in a window, scaled on all processors. At 800x600 the viewer's window is
# the first extra one: content 640x400 at 38,62; the 800x600 screen fits as
# 533x400 (66%) with dark bands of 53 pixels left and right.
@cpus 4
@ai
$ ai control on
$ display mode 800x600
~ ^mode: 800x600
$ screenshot /screen.bmp
saved the screen (800 x 600) to /screen.bmp (1406 KiB)
$ view /screen.bmp
/screen.bmp (800 x 600 pixels) in a new window: q leaves
$ sleep 1
@screenshot 800x600 pixels 60,200=202020 660,200=202020 95,66=a8d0f0
$ threads
~ view +(waiting|ready)
@ctl keys {machine} "q"
$ sleep 1
$ threads
!~ view
# The same as a PNG: written here, read back by the viewer (beenet::png).
$ screenshot /screen.png
saved the screen (800 x 600) to /screen.png (1407 KiB)
$ view /screen.png
/screen.png (800 x 600 pixels) in a new window: q leaves
$ sleep 1
@screenshot 800x600 pixels 60,200=202020 660,200=202020 95,66=a8d0f0
@ctl keys {machine} "q"
$ sleep 1
# GIF: one picture of the screen, read back by the viewer (beenet::gif).
$ screenshot /screen.gif
~ ^saved the screen \(800 x 600\) to /screen\.gif \(\d+ KiB\)$
$ view /screen.gif
/screen.gif (800 x 600 pixels) in a new window: q leaves
$ sleep 1
@screenshot 800x600 pixels 60,200=202020 660,200=202020
@ctl keys {machine} "q"
$ sleep 1
# A recording of the screen while the monitor (new numbers every second)
# is open: several different pictures, and the viewer plays them.
$ monitor
the system monitor in a new window: q leaves
$ record /clip.gif 3
~ ^recorded 1[0-5] pictures in [34] s \([2-9]\d* different\) to /clip\.gif \(\d+ KiB\)$
@ctl keys {machine} "q"
$ sleep 1
$ view /clip.gif
~ ^/clip\.gif \(800 x 600 pixels, [2-9]\d* frames\) in a new window: space pauses, n steps, q leaves$
$ sleep 1
@screenshot 800x600 pixels 60,200=202020 660,200=202020
$ threads
~ view +(waiting|ready)
@ctl keys {machine} " "
@ctl keys {machine} "n"
@ctl keys {machine} "q"
$ sleep 1
$ threads
!~ view
$ record /clip.txt 3
usage: record FILE.gif SECONDS   (1 to 60; Ctrl+C stops early)
$ record /clip.gif 0
usage: record FILE.gif SECONDS   (1 to 60; Ctrl+C stops early)
# What is no picture opens no window.
$ echo hello > /note.txt
$ view /note.txt
view /note.txt: not a picture this OS can show (PNG, GIF, BMP or PPM)
$ view /missing.bmp
view /missing.bmp: no such file or directory
$ view
usage: view FILE   (a PNG, GIF, BMP or PPM picture; an animated GIF plays)
$ screenshot
usage: screenshot FILE   (saves the screen as a picture: PNG or GIF if FILE ends in .png or .gif, BMP otherwise)
